lt of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can list its possible values in a sequence: $0,1,2,3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73762ed-d70e-505e-83c9-a45a7bb49679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358959} {:answer/id #uuid "70acd9eb-db10-59f5-9f24-6577e58acdf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358960} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c0cc6f-bd17-5d4d-9a59-e2f52551ace2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358961} {:answer/id #uuid "eff4a979-10a7-5ebe-bd4d-70dc5129cf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358962} {:answer/id #uuid "6017175a-b713-5849-9fb4-6e903d4a6a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eff4a979-10a7-5ebe-bd4d-70dc5129cf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358962} :answer-field/id #uuid "15bd96ad-d876-5a6d-b5da-db132950ed3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f10ce05b-98d5-5803-ada2-520a875a4943" :question/math-academy-id "q-83761" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The weight of a randomly selected student\n- The amount of water in a randomly selected student's water bottle\n- The number of books borrowed by a randomly selected library user in a particular month" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, we cannot list the possible real-numbered values in a sequence.\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
